==============================================================================================================================
L22 -- the solenoidal (curl) part of the QUMOND field in clusters, and whether it moves L2
==============================================================================================================================

  inputs: 248 corrected X-COP rows, 12 clusters, radii [30, 40, 50, 75, 100, 150, 200, 300, 420, 750, 1000] kpc; footings {'canonical': 9.3619e-11, 'alt': 1.1279e-10}
          source: closure_2026/cluster_measurement_audit_2026/results.json (the lead's radius-unit audit)

  the spherically averaged baryon profile used for the geometry runs (median of the seven X-COP
  clusters that ship a stellar profile; monotone log-log interpolation, end log-slopes 1.04 inward / 1.34 outward, smooth truncation at 3000 kpc):
      30kpc 3.74e+11Msun  75kpc 1.21e+12Msun  200kpc 6.01e+12Msun  750kpc 4.56e+13Msun

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  PART A -- the two solvers, validated
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] V0 [control] solver A reproduces the analytic Hernquist Newtonian field over 1 kpc - 10 Mpc to better than 0.5%   (max relative error 2.45e-03 (a0-independent; solver A, l_max = 24))
  [PASS] V1 [control] solver A reproduces the analytic Miyamoto-Nagai in-plane Newtonian force for a FLATTENED source (a = 4 kpc, b = 0.3 kpc, axis ratio 0.075) over 3b - 100 kpc to better than 0.5%   (max relative error 3.24e-03 for r > 3b = 0.9 kpc (l_max = 400, 811 Gauss-Legendre nodes in mu); STATED LIMIT: between b and 3b the same expansion reaches 1.0e-01, because a Legendre series in cos(theta) resolves a disc poorly at radii inside its own half-thickness. Every galaxy model below carries its own version of this check (V7))
  [PASS] V2 [control] for a SPHERICAL source the solved QUMOND field equals the algebraic nu(g_N) g_N to better than 0.3% and the solenoidal part vanishes to better than 1e-3 of the total   (canonical: |solved/algebraic - 1| max 1.6e-03, |a_S|/|a| max 2.1e-04, alt: |solved/algebraic - 1| max 1.6e-03, |a_S|/|a| max 2.1e-04)
  [PASS] V3 [control] the Newtonian limit: with the source scaled up by 1e6 so that g_N >> a0 everywhere, the solved field returns g_N to better than 0.5%   (canonical: max |solved/g_N - 1| = 2.3e-03 at min s = 1.4e+03, alt: max |solved/g_N - 1| = 2.3e-03 at min s = 1.2e+03)

  V4  the closed-loop test.  L1 measured that the ALGEBRAIC multiplier does net work around a loop once the
      Newtonian field stops being radial (5.7e-3 of the path integral for g04k's rule).  The solved QUMOND field
      is the gradient of a potential BY CONSTRUCTION, so this check verifies the implementation, not the physics.
      |circulation|/int|F|dl on a 100 x 90 kpc loop in a q = 0.6 cluster: Newtonian 3.45e-06, ALGEBRAIC nu(|g_N|)g_N 9.38e-03, SOLVED QUMOND 4.30e-06
  [PASS] V4 [control] the SOLVED field is conservative -- its loop integral sits at the same floor as the exactly conservative Newtonian field on the same loop, and at least 500x below the ALGEBRAIC multiplier's, which exceeds 1e-3, reproducing L1's defect   (solved 4.3e-06, Newtonian 3.5e-06 (both are exact gradients; the residual is the cubic-spline interpolation floor of the loop evaluator), algebraic 9.4e-03, ratio 2179x (L1 measured 5.7e-3 for the g04k rule on a galaxy loop))

