  [PASS] V5 [control] the independent Cartesian FFT solver reproduces solver A's local solenoidal fraction to better than 20%, and its mass bias to within its own spherical-source floor, on the same oblate configuration   (max relative disagreement in |a_S|/|a| = 8.4% over 200-1200 kpc; max absolute disagreement in B - 1 = 8.6e-04 against solver B's own floor 6.2e-04 (the two solvers share no code: multipole Green's functions on a log-r/Legendre grid vs a zero-padded Cartesian FFT))
  [PASS] V6 [control] refining the multipole solver (l_max 32 -> 56, 900 -> 1400 radial points, 96 -> 160 angular nodes) moves the solenoidal fraction and the mass bias by less than 10%   (q = 0.7 at 100/300/1000 kpc: |a_S|/|a| moves by 0.1%, the departure of B from 1 by 2.6%)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  PART C -- the same correction on the galaxy side, which L2's ratio also contains
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  L2's C5 compares two MEASUREMENTS of Delta = (g_obs - g_bar)/a0 at the same s.  The curl field is a THEORY
  effect: it says the field the theory predicts from a given baryon distribution is not nu(g_N)g_N.  That applies
  to discs as well as clusters, and discs are far flatter than clusters, so the honest test corrects BOTH sides.
  Each SPARC galaxy is given a Miyamoto-Nagai disc matched to its baryonic mass and [3.6] disc scale length, with
  b/a = 0.15 (h_z/h_R for spirals); b/a = 0.075 and 0.30 are run as the systematic.  Modelling every galaxy as a
  pure disc, with no bulge and with the gas as flat as the stars, OVERSTATES the galaxy-side correction, which is
  the direction that would help the cluster ratio -- so the test is being generous to the rescue.

  SPARC: 144 galaxies, 2681 points (Q<3, i>30, eV/V<0.10) -- L2's own selection

  144 per-galaxy QUMOND solves x 4 settings done in 81s
    b/a = 0.150,       alt footing: in-plane B over the SPARC points, median 0.9770, 16-84 pct [0.9239, 0.9946], min 0.5164;  Newtonian check: median 6.0e-05, 95pct 3.9e-04, 0 of 2681 points over 3% and left uncorrected
    b/a = 0.075, canonical footing: in-plane B over the SPARC points, median 0.9790, 16-84 pct [0.9245, 0.9949], min 0.4950;  Newtonian check: median 2.0e-04, 95pct 1.2e-03, 2 of 2681 points over 3% and left uncorrected
    b/a = 0.150, canonical footing: in-plane B over the SPARC points, median 0.9783, 16-84 pct [0.9280, 0.9947], min 0.5205;  Newtonian check: median 6.0e-05, 95pct 3.9e-04, 0 of 2681 points over 3% and left uncorrected
    b/a = 0.300, canonical footing: in-plane B over the SPARC points, median 0.9776, 16-84 pct [0.9340, 0.9938], min 0.6393;  Newtonian check: median 2.4e-05, 95pct 2.6e-04, 0 of 2681 points over 3% and left uncorrected
  [PASS] V7 [control] each galaxy's own Miyamoto-Nagai model is solved accurately enough at the radii where its SPARC points sit: the Newtonian in-plane force matches the analytic MN force for 95% of points to better than 0.5%, and fewer than 2% of points have to be left uncorrected   (worst 95th-percentile error over the four settings 1.2e-03; at most 2 of 2681 points (0.1%) exceed 3% and are given B = 1 (no correction), all of them at r well inside the disc half-thickness)

  the sign is the same as the cluster's -- the true QUMOND field in a disc plane is WEAKER than the algebraic
  multiplier -- but the magnitude is larger, because a disc is far flatter than a cluster.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  PART D -- THE TEST: does the solenoidal field move L2's 2.2-5.1x cluster/galaxy discrepancy?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Correction algebra.  Write B = g_true/g_alg with g_alg = g_bar + a0 Delta(s).  The measured excess is then
      Delta_meas = (g_obs - g_bar)/a0 = B Delta(s) + (B-1) s,   so the kernel a single-valued theory must supply is
      Delta_req = [ Delta_meas + (1-B) s ] / B.
  Both populations are corrected with their own B.  B < 1 raises the required Delta on BOTH sides; the ratio moves
  only through the difference between the two corrections.

  reproduction of L2's C5 with this script's own machinery (canonical footing, stellar-7 subset):
