  [FAIL] T1 [THE TEST] including the solenoidal field, on both sides, changes L2's cluster/galaxy boost ratio by more than 20% somewhere in the measured cluster axis-ratio range   (largest change in any bin, over q = 0.9-0.5, disc b/a = 0.075-0.30, both footings and both cluster subsets: +8.6%; headline (q = 0.7, b/a = 0.15) median -3.4%)
  [PASS] T2 [sign] the correction REDUCES the cluster/galaxy discrepancy rather than increasing it   (headline median change -3.4%; the cluster and galaxy corrections have the SAME sign (both fields are weaker than the algebraic multiplier) and the galaxy one is the larger, so the ratio falls slightly while the ABSOLUTE requirement on both rises)
  [FAIL] T3 [rescue] the correction closes at least half of L2's gap, i.e. the smallest cluster/galaxy ratio falls to under 1.6 from 2.2   (smallest ratio anywhere: 2.25 before, 2.20 after; 3.9% of the excess over unity removed)

  THE CONVERSE, determined rather than assumed.  The sign of the correction is NEGATIVE on both sides: the true
  QUMOND field is WEAKER than the algebraic multiplier, because nu(|g_N|)|g_N| is concave and the angular spread
  of |g_N| over a sphere therefore lowers its mean.  So the boost clusters REQUIRE goes UP, by +0.27% (median) and +1.16% (worst) across q = 0.9-0.5.
  The discrepancy ratio nonetheless falls slightly, only because the same effect is LARGER for the flatter discs
  on the other side of the comparison.  Neither move is anywhere near the factor of 2 a rescue would need.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THIS SETTLES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Settled.  L2's inversion assumed the curl field away by using Gauss's theorem on a sphere.  That assumption is
  not approximately right, it is EXACTLY right for the quantity L2 uses: div a_S = 0 forces the flux of the
  solenoidal field through every sphere to vanish, verified here to 3e-04 at axis ratios from 1.0 to 0.4
  and in merging configurations.  The caveat in FINDINGS.md is discharged.
  Real but small.  What geometry does change is the nonlinear angular average of the algebraic field itself,
  0.26% at q = 0.7 and 0.97% at q = 0.5, in the direction that makes clusters need MORE, not less.
  Genuinely large, locally.  The solenoidal field is not negligible as a FIELD: a few per cent of the total in a
  flattened cluster and tens of per cent inside a merging pair.  Any orbit integration, any lensing map, any
  non-radial dynamical calculation in this programme must solve for it -- which is exactly L1's warning.  It is
  only the sphere-averaged radial component, and hence the hydrostatic mass, that is protected.
  Not settled here.  The hydrostatic estimator's own triaxiality bias -- an observer azimuthally averaging X-ray
  data does not form the plain sphere average -- is a MEASUREMENT systematic common to every gravity theory, not
  a curl effect; the emission-weighted column above gives its size in this model and it is under a per cent at the
  gas axis ratios, but a proper treatment needs projection and a real emissivity, which this lane does not do.

